WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.620
Ju Shardlow: These eyes aren't real.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.620
In fact, they're contact lenses,

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.527
and it takes a very precise,

00:00:12.527 --> 00:00:15.490
skilled type of artist to make them.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.430
Today, we're going to
find out how they're made

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:21.040
by Eyeworks for Film.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.729
And they've made contact
lenses for "James Bond,"

00:00:23.729 --> 00:00:26.610
"Star Wars," and even "Game of Thrones."

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.520
We're gonna see how they're produced here

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:32.028
and even get fitted with
some lenses of our own.

00:00:32.028 --> 00:00:35.930
Clip: Such heavy ones
to distort such eyes.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.860
But Dr. Reuben Greenspoon of
Beverly Hills, California....

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:42.030
Ju: Beverly Hills
optometrist Reuben Greenspoon

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.440
is credited with first using lenses

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.150
to augment a character's appearance.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.700
Greenspoon was tasked with
changing an actor's eyes

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.700
from natural brown to blue in
1939's "Miracles for Sale."

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:01.170
He created a blue ceramic
material, which was glazed.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:04.130
The lens was attached
and run through a kiln

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:07.270
to fuse the glass and ceramic together,

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.453
leaving a round aperture of 4 millimeters.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.900
Since hydrogel soft contact lenses

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.100
went into mass production in 1971,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.470
lenses with simple color pigments

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.700
have become pretty accessible,

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.160
but designing and painting bespoke lenses

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.161
is still an art form.

00:01:27.161 --> 00:01:29.050
"Breaking Dawn" clip: This will
irritate your eyes at first.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.020
Ju: Actors wear contacts

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.340
to change their color
pigment to look older,

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.456
create imperfections,

00:01:36.456 --> 00:01:39.970
or even transform into another species.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:42.700
This is Sinead Sweeney,
one of the directors

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.670
of Eyeworks for Film.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.530
The company's worked on
some big Hollywood movies.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:49.923
Sinead Sweeney: The
idea of the contact lens

00:01:49.923 --> 00:01:52.090
will either come from the makeup designer

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:53.920
or the prosthetic department

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.790
or occasionally just from the director.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:59.680
So, they will approach us
maybe with a character brief

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.700
or just an idea, in which
case we would research

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.780
a lot about that look and then develop

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.490
some concept art, some designs.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.190
Ju: First, Sinead has got to check

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:12.490
that the actor's eyes
are suitable for lenses.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:13.970
She does a prescription check.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.497
U, A,

00:02:16.497 --> 00:02:17.371
F.

00:02:17.371 --> 00:02:19.580
She then checks the inside of the retina

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:22.970
with an ophthalmoscope
and takes a 3D scan.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:24.845
Sinead: So, we wanna
make sure that the lens

00:02:24.845 --> 00:02:26.861
sits exactly over the cornea

00:02:26.861 --> 00:02:29.077
so that when you're
wearing the contact lens

00:02:29.077 --> 00:02:30.680
or when our actors are wearing them,

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.030
that it feels very, very normal.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.760
Ju: Further checks look
at the curve of the eye,

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.525
the diameter of the iris,

00:02:37.525 --> 00:02:39.940
and the front of the eye for dryness,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:43.045
using a yellow dye called fluorescein.

00:02:43.045 --> 00:02:44.712
Are my eyes glowing?

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.210
I thought about actors on set,

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.530
you're on set for a really long time,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.070
and how do you stop your
eyes from getting really dry?

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:55.840
Sinead: We insert and
remove the lenses for them,

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.410
because their hands might
be covered in grease paint

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.260
or blood. And so then we monitor them,

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.380
we put drops in throughout the day.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.370
And we only limit the
wearing time, really.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:07.370
We don't allow them to wear them too long.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:09.990
Ju: There's a trick to
putting large lenses in.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.990
Chin up, then eyes down to the left.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:16.420
You feel like you have an
entire film over your eye now,

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.240
so I can picture that
would really put someone

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.360
into character quite quickly.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.690
Sinead: Yeah.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.860
Ju: The lenses take two
to three weeks to paint

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.540
and manufacture, but for
the purposes of filming,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.673
here are some that we made earlier.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.040
We're gonna return in just a bit

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.237
to see how these ones turn out.

00:03:36.498 --> 00:03:39.790
Oh, my...!

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:44.130
Manufacturers like No7 make
the prescription lenses,

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.630
and then a lens artist,

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.093
such as Cristina
Patterson from Eye Ink Fx,

00:03:49.093 --> 00:03:53.173
paints them by hand
using FDA-approved paint.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:58.370
The lenses are then
shipped and applied on set.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.800
Sinead: Subtlety is key
when it comes to the makeup,

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.510
so even one brushstroke too
far can make a real difference.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.410
Some really, really special
and specific pigments

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.243
that they use.

00:04:08.243 --> 00:04:10.350
Obviously, anything that
you're gonna put on a lens

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:14.270
that is ultimately gonna
go into someone's eye

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.190
really isn't just any pigment.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:18.560
There are a lot of safety guidelines,

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.890
and also the lenses are sterilized

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:22.490
with extremely fine brushes, yeah,

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:24.720
and you have to have
quite an amazing artist

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.800
in order to deliver
certain textures and looks.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.608
The detail is extremely fine.

00:04:33.233 --> 00:04:35.202
Ju: Oh, my...!

00:04:35.202 --> 00:04:37.002
Oh, my goodness!

00:04:37.893 --> 00:04:40.335
I look like a fish!

00:04:40.335 --> 00:04:44.017
I look like a fish! I'm
a fish! I am Aquaman.

00:04:44.017 --> 00:04:47.090
I am what Aquaman was
supposed to look like.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:49.010
Sinead: It's very similar,
actually, in design

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:51.780
to a lot of the "Star Trek" lenses.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.370
These lenses were painted
by the same artist.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:58.330
Ju: I've really got that
kind of bug-eyed, fishy look

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:01.740
going on, but there is
something really alien about it.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.020
The detail here is incredible.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:04.930
The most strange feeling,

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.310
'cause they feel really jelly-like

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:09.810
but they look so glazed, like ceramic.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:12.110
The lenses flatten to your eye,

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.690
so it's important to
get rid of air bubbles

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:17.140
and keep putting in drops.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.480
Sinead: If we're gonna sell
this as an actual creature,

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.330
the pupil would always
be in the same place.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.530
So we make sure that
the lens is stabilized.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:25.800
I think one of the biggest
issues we have with that

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.490
is actually when someone is lying down,

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.560
because the lenses are weighted,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.840
so they tend to wanna fall with
gravity to the lowest point.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.040
So if you can imagine
you've got your lenses

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.300
sitting like a cat's eye, for example,

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.800
and then they lie down,
everything kind of swings.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.730
Ju: And they're not the
only lenses in the building.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.830
Sinead keeps cases of celebrity eyes

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:50.868
from productions the company's worked on.

00:05:50.868 --> 00:05:54.020
Sinead: Serpent or
snake-eye looks that we used

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.940
on David Tennant on "Good Omens."

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:58.070
So, we've got our Night King.

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:00.660
When you see them in this show,

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.110
they have a post-glow,
which is added in CG.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.400
And also we put some
pixelated points on there,

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.880
which, with VFX, they could work with that

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.020
to pull the glow effect out.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:12.693
They weren't the original blue.

00:06:15.538 --> 00:06:17.050
Who else have we got in here?

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:18.880
Ju: I love the fact
you're saying "who else,"

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.263
like it's just, like, a collection

00:06:20.263 --> 00:06:21.246
of very dishy actors.
Sinead: To me,

00:06:21.246 --> 00:06:24.090
they're all people.

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:27.270
Ju: The company has made
lenses for

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:29.260
"Penny Dreadful" and "Spectre."

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.937
These are the Mountain's from "Game of Thrones."

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.320
Wow, that was a fascinating process.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.420
I can't believe that my
eyes looked like that.

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:42.950
Into that celebrity eye graveyard

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:44.270
that we saw at the end,

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.780
if my contact lenses
can make it into there,

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.508
I would be very, very happy.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.850
It's clear the industry
has come a long way

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:57.210
since just being able to
change eye color in 1939.

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:00.050
Now, thanks to this movie magic,

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:03.467
actors can become all sorts of creatures.

